+{"status": "in_progress", "id": "QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-8", "title": "[P1] test_user_modify_rule_wraps_in_unshare fails on any host with subuid/subgid ranges — asserts exact unshare prefix without --map-users/--map-groups flags", "detail": "plugin/test_interruptor.py::TestUserRules::test_user_modify_rule_wraps_in_unshare asserts result.modified.startswith(\"unshare --user --pid --fork --kill-child=SIGKILL bash -c \") but plugin/terminal_jail/interruptor/userns.py prepends --map-users=65534:<subuid>:1 --map-groups=65534:<subgid>:1 -S 65534 -G 65534 when /etc/subuid has an entry for the running user. On bunker-las-02 agent (uid bunker-135a5acd, subuid range 493216) the actual payload was \"unshare --user --map-users=65534:493216:1 --map-groups=65534:493216:1 -S 65534 -G 65534 --pid --fork --kill-child=SIGKILL bash -c ...\" — assertion fails. Passes on dev box (no subuid entry for kara). Same failure in ci-pass native leg (1 failed, 560 passed, 5 skipped) and chaos-resource (identical). This is a test brittleness defect: the suite is red on any multi-user Linux host with subuid/subgid allocation.", "priority": "P1", "source": "qa-foreman", "reasoning": {"note": "QA foreman cycle 2026-09-18 — fresh-system CI pass + chaos on bunker-las-02 (ci-pass, chaos-resource)"}, "review_notes": "Evidence: /tmp/bunker-qa-evidence-terminal-jail-20260918-r2.jsonl cells ci-pass+chaos-resource. Repro: run pytest plugin/test_interruptor.py::TestUserRules::test_user_modify_rule_wraps_in_unshare on any user with a /etc/subuid entry. Fix: assert with a regex or check that the payload contains \"--pid --fork --kill-child=SIGKILL bash -c\" rather than startswith on the full prefix, or mock the subuid lookup.", "ts": "2026-09-18T12:38:18.740706+00:00", "worker_status": "dispatched", "foreman_note": "Tick #295 selected: highest-value unblocked P1 QA regression; live premise reproduced in board evidence; one worker dispatch planned."}
 {"status": "complete", "id": "DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-15", "title": "[dogfood:P1] Auto-sandbox on uid-mapping hosts rewrites commands into a launch that cannot read the caller's own files (payload = caller's subuid, not the caller)", "detail": "plugin/terminal_jail/interruptor/userns.py::unshare_prefix() selects the MAPPED prefix whenever mapped_launch_ok() is True, and that preflight is `unshare --user --map-users=65534:<subuid>:1 --map-groups=65534:<subgid>:1 -S 65534 -G 65534 --pid --fork --kill-child=SIGKILL true` — it proves the namespace can be CREATED, never that the payload can still reach the user's files. With -S 65534 -G 65534 + --map-users=<subuid> the payload's host uid is the caller's SUBUID, so DAC denies the caller's home (drwx------). Verified live on a fresh ephemeral Debian 13 host (bunker-las-02, subuid 1803936): `cd ~/terminal-jail && terminal-jail python3 scripts/pidns-capability-probe.py` (the quickstart's own §3a Step-1 command, through the tool) -> `python3: can't open file '/home/bunker-<id>/terminal-jail/scripts/pidns-capability-probe.py': [Errno 13] Permission denied` rc=2, while the direct run prints FULL; `terminal-jail python3 ~/writer.py` -> Permission denied rc=2; script copied to world-readable /tmp -> runs, then its $HOME and repo writes fail PermissionError(13) with rc=0 (partial SILENT failure). Hand-run control: the mapped form cannot `ls` the caller's file, the legacy form reads it and the script runs/writes. The project's dev host is DEGRADED (AppArmor denies uid mapping) so only the legacy branch is ever exercised in CI, tests and prior dogfood runs — the defect is invisible there. This is the runtime consequence of the same root cause QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-8 reports as test brittleness; that row's fix (regex the assertion) would leave the runtime defect in place.", "priority": "P1", "source": "dogfood", "reasoning": {"note": "dogfood run 2026-09-18 (cron, terminal-jail) — real-use verification against HEAD 5275a86; install leg on ephem